@flexiarg baldwin/two-claims-not-one
@title Separate Evolutionary Guidance from Genetic Assimilation
@sigils [🌏/分]
@keywords baldwin-effect, genetic-assimilation, plasticity, learnability, claim-boundary
@audience experiment designers, evolutionary theorists
@tone discriminating
@style pattern
@childof baldwin/mechanism
@references [futon-theory/baldwin-cycle baldwin/test-evolution-of-learnability-before-static-assimilation]
@references-extra ["Crispo (2007), doi:10.1111/j.1558-5646.2007.00203.x"]

! conclusion:
  Report evolutionary guidance and genetic assimilation as distinct claims:
  guidance means that lifetime adaptation changes selection on inherited traits;
  assimilation means that inherited causes replace an environmentally or
  developmentally induced contribution and plastic dependence decreases.

  + context:
    The literature uses "Baldwin effect" both broadly for learning-guided evolution
    and narrowly for a rise-and-fall of learning. Crispo distinguishes Baldwinian
    organic selection and genetic accommodation from genetic assimilation, noting
    that Baldwinian evolution can maintain or increase plasticity.

  + IF:
    An experiment contains an evolutionary outer loop and a plastic inner loop.

  + HOWEVER:
    A definition that demands a fully static endpoint will classify evolution of
    faster learning as failure, while a definition that merely observes high fitness
    after learning will classify ordinary learning as evolutionary guidance.

  + THEN:
    Require a between-generation counterfactual for guidance: comparable evolution
    with versus without lifetime adaptation. Require an additional dependence decline
    and inherited-function test for assimilation. Label complete static replacement as
    strong assimilation rather than silently making it the only Baldwin criterion.

  + BECAUSE:
    Selection can favor an inherited morphology, initialization, prior, or learning
    rule because it makes adaptation faster without ever making adaptation dispensable.
    The causal influence of learning on evolution is already present; loss of
    plasticity is a separate evolutionary response to its costs and environmental
    predictability.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If the evolved population learns no faster and performs no better than an
      evolution-only control, continued plastic success is not evidence of Baldwinian
      guidance. If guidance occurs but disabling learning destroys performance, the
      guidance claim may pass while assimilation fails.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Add separate `GuidanceWitness` and `AssimilationWitness` outcomes to the Lean model.
    - Preserve `BaldwinDesign.BaldwinWitness` as the strong complete-assimilation witness.
